Early Days (Before SOAP & REST)
In the early days, communication between client and server was difficult because data was exchanged in binary formats, which were not human-readable and hard to debug.

🔹 SOAP Introduction
To solve this problem, Microsoft introduced SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol).
SOAP uses XML messaging, which is:
Human-readable
Machine-readable
Because of this, SOAP became popular.
SOAP Features
SOAP is a protocol
Uses strict standards
Built-in security
Built-in ACID properties
Supports WS-Security
Very reliable for enterprise applications
SOAP Limitations
Supports only XML (no JSON or other formats)
XML is heavy and verbose
Requires lengthy and complex code
Uses action-based URLs
Example (SOAP): saveTeacher,getTeacher,updateTeacher

To overcome SOAP’s complexity, REST (Representational State Transfer) was introduced.
REST is not a protocol — it is an architectural style.

